1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
A Solana signature is useful evidence, but by itself it is hard to read.
ReceiptLens turns Devnet data into a compact, human-readable receipt.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Enter a public Solana Devnet transaction signature.
ReceiptLens is read-only: no wallet, key entry, signing, or submission.

3
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:48,000
The result is finalized: slot 505834809, block time 2026-09-30 08:00:35 UTC.
Fee 15,500 lamports, 24,547 compute units, 10 accounts, 1 signer, 15 logs.

4
00:00:48,000 --> 00:01:08,000
ReceiptLens identifies ComputeBudget, Associated Token Account, Tokenkeg, and Memo.
The sample shows 164,398,058 SPL token base units and a separate -0.0000155 SOL fee.

5
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:28,000
The product is deliberately conservative and does not guess missing metadata.
Unsafe balances and failed token instructions are omitted; fees and rent remain visible.

6
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:45,000
This is public Solana Devnet test activity, not real income or payment proof.
An instruction amount is not claimed as a net recipient credit.

7
00:01:45,000 --> 00:02:05,000
ReceiptLens reports public RPC evidence at inspection time.
A finalized transaction does not prove a real-world commercial obligation was satisfied.

8
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:20,000
The static MVP uses getTransaction and getSignatureStatuses at fixed public Devnet RPC.
No custom program ID. Local tests and setup instructions are included.

9
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:30,000
ReceiptLens: read a Solana transaction like a receipt.
Try the public Devnet MVP and inspect the source. No wallet, signing, payment, or submission flow.
